  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to differ on the \nmagnitude and positioning of the subtropical ridge early next week\nwhich will dictate how hot temperatures get and how soon rain \nchances will return to our area. Both the EPS and GEFS show the \nridge strengthening over the Southern Plains through the middle of\nthe week which will allow hghts aloft to increase across much of \nthe Desert Southwest. In turn, temperatures will also increase \nfrom near normal Monday to slightly above normal on Tuesday. The \ntemperature forecast becomes much more unclear heading into the \nmiddle of next week with large spreads in the NBM interquartile \nrange. This is likely due to differences in the magnitude of the \nridge with many EPS members showing a stronger ridge with 500 mb \nhghts approaching 596-598 dam while the GEFS shows slightly lower \noverall hghts. For now NBM temperatures show a cooling trend after\nTuesday which is likely due to an increase in moisture as \nsoutheasterly flow aloft returns to the lower deserts. \n\nPWATs are expected to increase back to around 1.5-1.8\" by the middle \nof next week which will bring rain chances back into the picture by \nthe Wednesday-Thursday timeframe when PoPs increase to around 20-40% \nareawide. The GFS is even showing an easterly wave/inverted trough \narriving into southern AZ along with additional tropical Pacific \nmoisture on Wednesday which could boost rain chances significantly,\nbut we will likely have to wait for more run to run consistency \nas these features are always difficult to resolve at longer time \nscales. Either way, most of the GEFS and EPS members are showing \nmeasurable precipitation across portions of the area by the middle\nof next week.",
